/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  postDict -- STAGE C ONLY.  Stages A and B carry no postDict at all, and that
  absence is the sequence's invariant working:

      A STAGE MAY NOT COST WHAT IT DID NOT SIZE.

  =========================================================================
  WHY THE CONVERTER'S VOLUME IS NOT TYPED IN
  =========================================================================
  The flagship green-ammonia plant and `ammonia02_full_plant` both size this
  converter with `designRules { volume <m3>; }` and put where the number came
  from in a `//` comment beside it.  The design sheet then records the basis
  as "volume (author-set)" and says nothing about a space velocity, because
  there is nothing about a space velocity for it to read.  A fact a reader
  must act on, living in a comment, is the shape this project has now found
  five times.

  This case declares the RULE instead, in grammar the engine already parses
  (`src/postProcessing/sizing/VesselSize.cpp:83-85`):

      spaceVelocity  ->  V = Q_gas / SV      basis "catalyst V = Q/SV"

  so the volume becomes an ENGINE OUTPUT computed from this run's own
  converter throughput, the rule reaches `design/<unit>/<tag>` in the sheet's
  own `basis` field, and the volume tracks the flowsheet instead of standing
  still while it moves.  `flowKey N_in_mol_s` puts the space velocity on the
  INLET gas, which is the convention every published GHSV uses; the sizer's
  own default is the OUTLET (`N_out_mol_s`), which for this reaction is 22 %
  smaller because four moles become two.

  =========================================================================
  THE BASIS OF THE NUMBER, AND THE TRAP IN IT -- READ THIS BEFORE COPYING
  =========================================================================
  `VesselSize` builds Q_gas as `N * R * T / P` at THE UNIT'S OWN (T, P) --
  700 K and 200 bar here.  So its `spaceVelocity` is on ACTUAL gas volume,
  and `[1/h]` is the honest unit for it.

  EVERY SPACE VELOCITY IN THE AMMONIA LITERATURE IS QUOTED ON NORMAL OR
  STANDARD GAS VOLUME -- Nm3 of gas per m3 of catalyst per hour.  Typing an
  industrial GHSV straight into this key is wrong by the density ratio, which
  at these conditions is

      Q_normal / Q_actual = (T_n * P_r) / (P_n * T_r)
                          = (273.15 K * 2.0e7 Pa) / (101325 Pa * 700 K)
                          = 77.022

  -- a factor of 77, at exit 0, producing a plausible-looking volume.  The
  engine has no grammar for declaring which basis a space velocity is on, and
  nothing in it can detect the substitution.  That is a finding, recorded
  here and reported, not worked around.

  So the value below is stated in THE ENGINE'S OWN BASIS and the arithmetic
  that produced it is here rather than in a reader's head:

      20000 Nm3/(m3.h)  /  77.022  =  259.665 h^-1   (actual gas, 700 K, 200 bar)

  WHERE 20000 Nm3/(m3.h) COMES FROM: IT IS A DECLARED ASSUMPTION.  Those are
  the words.  The only primary source retrieved for this case that states a
  range is US 4,568,530 (Mandelik, Cassata, Shires & van Dijk, MW Kellogg,
  4 February 1986), "Ammonia synthesis", which says the catalyst space
  velocities "are generally between 5000 and 150,000 m3 per hour of gas per
  m3 of catalyst at standard conditions".  That band is a factor of 30 wide
  and pins nothing; it brackets 20000 and does not source it.  The patent
  does not say WHICH standard conditions, either, which is the same basis
  ambiguity one level up.  That quotation also reached here through a
  fetch-and-summarise of the patent page rather than a read of the raw
  document -- weaker evidence than the approach-to-equilibrium sentences
  quoted in the flowsheetDict, which were read out of the retrieved text
  itself.

  WHAT WOULD REPLACE IT: an integrated rate law with a pellet effectiveness
  factor, which is stage D of this sequence and which the engine cannot run
  today -- `AmmoniaSynthesisRate` is included by its own .cpp and by a props
  bench, and by no reactor.  Until then this volume is a feasibility-class
  number: enough to cost a vessel, not enough to build one.

  =========================================================================
  WHAT IS SIZED, AND WHAT IS NOT
  =========================================================================
  Sized: the CONVERTER (catalyst vessel, space velocity), the high-pressure
  SEPARATOR and the product LET-DOWN drum (both on vapour residence time).
  NOT sized, and so not costed: there are no compressors and no exchangers in
  this flowsheet at all -- the loop is isobaric at 200 bar and the makeup
  compression and feed/effluent interchange are folded into the unit duties,
  which is the simplification this topology inherits from
  `tutorials/steady/flowsheets/ammonia01_synthesis_loop`.  So this CAPEX is
  PARTIAL IN UNITS, not only in provenance, and it is smaller than a real
  loop's by the two largest rotating machines.  Read `ammonia02_full_plant`
  for a loop that carries them.

  `corrosionAllow` and `jointEfficiency` are DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED here.
  The engine supplies both from `sizing/DesignDefaults`, announces each one at
  its site and in the end-of-run caveat block, and lists them in the design
  sheet's `assumed ( ... )` block -- which is precisely the difference between
  a number this case chose and a number the engine chose.  Declaring them
  would silence that, and this is the one case in the corpus whose subject is
  that distinction.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
sizing
{
    units
    (
        //  THE CONVERTER.  The volume is NOT here; the RULE is.
        { unitName converter;  type vessel;  material SS316;
          designRules { spaceVelocity 259.665;      // 1/h, ACTUAL gas at 700 K / 200 bar
                        flowKey N_in_mol_s;         // GHSV is on the INLET gas
                        L_over_D 6.0;
                        pressureDesign 220.0; } }

        //  The high-pressure SEPARATOR: a knock-out drum, 45 s of vapour holdup.
        { unitName separator;  type vessel;  material SS316;
          designRules { residenceTime 45.0;  flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3.0;
                        pressureDesign 220.0; } }

        //  The product LET-DOWN drum at 20 bar: smaller, and carbon steel.
        { unitName letdown;    type vessel;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { residenceTime 60.0;  flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3.0;
                        pressureDesign 25.0; } }
    );
}
costing
{
    method    Turton;
    year      2026;
    cepci     820;
    cepci2001 397;
    usdToEur  0.92;
}
